  <h2>Why I brought back low-lift audio pop-ups (instead of another big summit)</h2>
  <p>I could have made this a full summit. Video interviews, speaker pages, everyone recording a 30-minute presentation, I've done that before, and there's nothing wrong with it. But I'm in a season of asking myself how I can get the result without making the thing enormous.</p>
  <p>So I went back to something I already knew worked: Voxer-style interviews. I send a voice note question, the guest answers when they have time, I reply when I have time, and it goes back and forth asynchronously. No calendar tetris between Australia and the US, no 5am Zoom calls. I download the audio, clean it up a little, and that's basically the whole production process.</p>
  <p>I actually like that it doesn't sound perfectly polished. There are pauses, background noise, someone stumbling over a word. It sounds like two business friends sending voice notes back and forth, and you happen to have access to the conversation. When the topic is something like real Black Friday numbers, I want it to feel exactly like that, not a conference stage.</p>

  <h2>The real numbers from my first pop-up (the good and the humbling)</h2>
  <p>The August audience growth event wrapped up last week, and here's what actually happened. 303 people signed up for the free event, with eight speakers, each sending just one email. The podcast feed picked up 370+ downloads, not counting the people who listened straight from the webpage.</p>
  <p>Then there's the season pass, lifetime access to all five events for $97. I announced it on opening night to roughly 100 people. I sold one. Just one. (Thank you, Colie.)</p>
  <blockquote>"Was making $10,000 the job I gave this event? Because if it wasn't, that's kind of a weird scorecard to keep."</blockquote>
  <p>I could have left that number out and just told you about the 303 signups. But if we're actually talking business, the useful question is whether it made money, and mostly, it didn't. That doesn't mean the event failed. It means I was judging it against a job I never gave it in the first place.</p>

  <h2>What a "free" event was actually for</h2>
  <p>I built these pop-ups to do three things: nurture my list with something fun and different, test a repeatable event format that doesn't wipe me out, and warm people up for my Break Up With Instagram mini-course launch.</p>
  <p>On the first two, it delivered. On the third, about 30% of the people who bought the mini-course had listened to the event first, real overlap, but I'd have liked it higher. That tells me something useful: either the bridge between the event and the offer wasn't strong enough, or an event about audience growth in general was too broad a lead-in for an Instagram-specific offer. Both good things to know before I build the next one.</p>

  <h2>How I actually find new collaborators</h2>
  <p>The question I get most since hosting this event is where I find people to collaborate with. The truth is I deliberately avoid working with the same 15 people over and over, otherwise every event just circulates the same audience back through itself.</p>
  <p>My biggest source is watching who else shows up in a bundle or summit I'm already part of, they've already said yes to promotion and borrowed audiences, so it's a warm pool. I'll follow them, read their emails, watch how they treat people, sometimes for six months before I ever pitch them anything. I also scroll with intention, looking for smart replies and different perspectives instead of doom-scrolling, and occasionally drop a callout in a collaboration Facebook group when I need a couple of fresh voices, which is exactly how I found two new guests for the next event.</p>
  <h3>Your network compounds too</h3>
  <p>Every summit, bundle or podcast guest spot puts you next to another group of business owners you didn't have access to before. You won't always be able to measure the value of a relationship in the week it starts, sometimes the real payoff shows up six months later.</p>

  <h2>Building a growth engine, not chasing one big win</h2>
  <p>One event isn't the story here, five of them, run back to back through the rest of the year, is. 303 people here, a podcast interview that brings 40 there, another event that brings 200, that's how a list actually compounds, without needing one single thing to be "the thing."</p>
  <p>The next one is the Black Friday Case Files, running September 14–18. I've invited business owners to share one Black Friday promotion each, the ones that worked and the ones that went horribly wrong, because there's just as much to learn from a promotion that made two sales as one that made $80K in four days.</p>

